WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.210
The Frozen easter egg everyone missed, secret
sacrifices, and my theory on the real reason

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.380
Olaf knows his name are just a few of the
biggest reveals in Once Upon A Snowman.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.350
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm explaining the biggest new Frozen details

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.650
we discovered in Olaf's short film on Disney
Plus.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.330
Once Upon A Snowman is a Frozen midquel that
reveals the origins of Olaf between the time

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.570
Elsa creates him and when he meets Anna and
Kristoff.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.690
The short film begins during "Let It Go",
and as Olaf comes to life we discover it's

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:35.540
actually Elsa's cape
and her iconic "the cold never bothered me

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.720
anyway" moment which sends him rolling down
the mountain setting him on the path that

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.720
eventually leads him to Anna.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.720
"I'm alive!

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.840
Who said that?"

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.640
After pondering the nature of his existence,
Olaf wanders over to Oaken's Trading Post.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.700
He hears Kristoff sing "Reindeer Are Better
Than People" and arrives just as Anna's leaving.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.000
As Olaf enters, we see Oaken putting up Anna's
coronation dress for sale,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.910
which reveals she must have been short of
cash and decided to trade her expensive royal

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.000
dress in return for the tools and carrots
Kristoff couldn't afford plus her winter attire.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.560
This also explains a missing detail from the
original movie as to why Anna left Oaken's

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.210
wearing a purple skirt rather than the green
one she had on originally.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.280
After entering the Trading Post, Olaf remarks
on the hidden meaning of its name:

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.359
"Wandering Oaken's?

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:26.130
Do you know that if you scramble up those
letters, it's an anagram for 'Naked Norwegians'?"

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.130
"Mmm?"

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.869
This fun little joke appears to be the secret
easter egg from the first Frozen movie that

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.279
Disney producer Peter Del Vecho said people
on the internet hadn't picked up on.

00:01:35.279 --> 00:01:39.009
And if you're thinking like me that Olaf didn't
learn to read until Frozen 2.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:40.350
"Okay, Olaf, you're up."

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.109
"So much easier now that I can read."

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:45.840
well Disney went right ahead and lampshaded
us by acknowledging this inconsistency.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.279
"And I can't even read.

00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:50.950
I genuinely don't know how I just did that."

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.329
At Oaken's, we also discover where Olaf's
love of summertime came from.

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:55.329
"Summer?"

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:56.329
"Mmm-hmm."

00:01:56.329 --> 00:01:57.329
"Oh.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:01:59.489
I don't know why, but I've always loved the
idea of summer."

00:01:59.489 --> 00:02:03.070
When the shopkeeper puts a stereoscope on
the little snowman's face and shows him a

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.070
series of pictures.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.070
"What is this?"

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.579
"That would be of summer."

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:08.579
"Summer?

00:02:08.579 --> 00:02:09.579
It's beautiful!"

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:14.290
There are some cool easter eggs here and,
interestingly, many of the images Olaf sees

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.880
appear later in the main Frozen movie when
he sings "In Summer".

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.200
For example, the red and orange parasol and
the green and white striped ball are the same

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.930
ones we see in the song when Olaf is imagining
himself sunbathing on the beach.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.470
And the slide with the bees and the purple
and yellow flowers,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:34.640
as well as the dandelions in this picture
all appear during this moment from "In Summer".

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.220
♪ Bees will buzz ♪
♪ Kids will blow dandelion fuzz ♪

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.250
This image of a meadow and bandstand is from
the moment in the song where Olaf sings together

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.600
with seagulls in a tribute to Bert from Mary
Poppins.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.820
And this red chequered blanket pops up in
the original film and again in the sequel.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.760
"I've never identified with anything so much
in my whole life.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.850
It's like snowmen in summer just belong together."

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.490
The short also reveals that Olaf was the original
cause of the wolf chase scene in the first

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.490
Frozen movie.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:08.040
The summer sausage that Oaken gives him for
a nose attracts unwanted attention from a

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.600
pack of wolves,
and as he attempts to escape the hungry horde,

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.540
he wooshes past behind Anna and Kristoff.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.660
That commotion seems to be why Kristoff stopped
the sled in the first movie and that caused

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.370
the wolf pack to notice him and Anna and switch
to chasing them instead of Olaf.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:28.470
It's during Olaf's escape from the wolves
that he first discovers how he can disassemble

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.940
and rearrange himself at will.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.940
"I'm falling apart.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.840
Is this a skill or a flaw?

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:35.840
Skill, skill!"

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.120
There's some great physical humour in these
scenes and we can see how he learns to control

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.510
his stick hands independently of his body,
a skill that we first saw him use when he

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.420
slapped Kristoff after meeting him in the
first film.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.680
"How does this work?

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.680
Ow!"

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.680
"Stop it, Sven.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:51.680
I'm trying to focus, here."

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.060
The short also reveals Olaf's noble and selfless
side when he decides to donate his sausage

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.480
nose to a hungry wolf.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.770
"You need this even more than I do."

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:04.330
Chronologically, this is the first example
of how Olaf is willing to sacrifice his own

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.600
wants and well-being for the sake of somebody
else,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.240
as he does later for Anna in the main movie
after she's been cruelly abandoned by Hans.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.750
"Love is putting someone else's needs before
yours."

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.320
"Olaf, you're melting."

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.279
"Some people are worth melting for."

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:23.060
And as a token of appreciation for Olaf's
sacrifice, the wolf gives the snowman a friendly

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.460
warm lick and a cuddle.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.729
"That felt just like a warm hug."

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:31.050
This is Olaf's first experience of the warm
hugs he's so fond of telling everyone about

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.389
in the Frozen movies.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.460
"I'm Olaf.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.759
And I like warm hugs."

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:35.759
"Good afternoon.

00:04:35.759 --> 00:04:36.759
I am Olaf, and...

00:04:36.759 --> 00:04:38.360
Please let me finish, and I like warm hugs."

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.479
"I like warm hugs."

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:44.939
And as Olaf thinks about that warm hug, we
discover that he's able to hear Elsa and Anna's

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:45.969
voices from years ago.

00:04:45.969 --> 00:04:47.410
"Do you wanna build a snowman?"

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.650
"Do the magic!"

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.779
These voices are from the first Frozen when
Elsa and Anna were little.

00:04:53.779 --> 00:04:54.779
"Do you want to build a snowman?"

00:04:54.779 --> 00:04:57.719
"Do the magic!"

00:04:57.719 --> 00:05:01.909
My theory on the reason why Olaf can hear
these voices from before he was even alive

00:05:01.909 --> 00:05:06.080
is because he's made of snow and ice which
are, of course, frozen water.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.979
And as was established in Frozen 2, water
has memory, and it seems that because Olaf

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:15.330
is made from Elsa's ice magic he's able to
connect to her memories.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:19.900
We then find out that this memory is how Olaf
discovers his name.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.789
"I'm Olaf, and I like warm hugs."

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:29.110
Basically, he's recalling the name that Elsa
gave to the snowman that she and Anna built

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:30.110
as kids.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.749
"Hi, I'm Olaf, and I like warm hugs."

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:37.990
As the short film ends we see Olaf spot Anna,
Kristoff and Sven near the frozen lake and

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.719
waterfall in the distance.

00:05:39.719 --> 00:05:43.889
He may be nose-less at this moment, but it
shows us how he ends up wandering over to

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:46.000
meet them in the first movie.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.340
And now when Anna gives him a carrot for a
nose in the main film, Olaf's reaction makes

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.240
even more sense after the events of Once Upon
A Snowman.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.259
"Are you okay?"

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:55.680
"Are you kidding me?

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.919
I am wonderful!

00:05:57.919 --> 00:05:59.469
I've always wanted a nose."

00:05:59.469 --> 00:06:03.319
So, did you like Olaf's new origin story in
Once Upon A Snowman?

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:06.099
And did you spot any other interesting details
in the short film?

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.449
Leave your thoughts in the comments below.

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:13.520
If you enjoyed these details, do leave a thumbs-up
and tap left for another Frozen video.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.770
Or tap right for something else you're sure
to like.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.729
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:19.009
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

